#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d116374b-b693-5d50-8019-fcc5a7766b9b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e0290b-b214-51b9-ae5b-fc3e291433d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266285} {:answer/id #uuid "4d569cb3-e268-51d7-8e1f-47a0e048ffa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266830} {:answer/id #uuid "50423a6e-7974-582d-a76f-cac676cf369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266858} {:answer/id #uuid "67f7f843-8d99-5b2c-a258-bfa87e57387b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267065} {:answer/id #uuid "bccbe989-ecf7-53c3-8c04-5337fd9a0320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50423a6e-7974-582d-a76f-cac676cf369c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266858} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82d9035-63ea-54b8-90da-3fbf50bd2ad3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c2ac9a-8e8a-51d7-84bb-f51632a65f59" :question/math-academy-id "q-139526" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are $15,$ $10,$ and $0,$ find the length of the ellipse's semi-major axis." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$\n\nSince the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ is $15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}=15\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=\\sqrt{15}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is $\\sqrt{15}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e50d71c6-f44a-5378-9963-36734ea464d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383419} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ca5674-a813-571b-805e-2032f3f85b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383420} {:answer/id #uuid "6475d879-742b-5337-8a8d-36ed47f16244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383421} {:answer/id #uuid "400156c4-1064-54a3-9f5b-1121fe453b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383422} {:answer/id #uuid "53eb8b86-520d-5c8a-ba6a-c003c2b2acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6475d879-742b-5337-8a8d-36ed47f16244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383421} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d030424-2f49-5472-a91f-15160f4718d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32d00c3f-b2c7-560e-b9fc-7da8d2b35ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-139534" :question/problem "A $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{5},$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd0e91f0-abbc-52e2-8d8b-94597e4892cd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Semi-Major Axis of the Image of the Unit Sphere Given the Corresponding Singular Values"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1c07e06-038d-5f20-b7aa-6a49d66cccc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383426} {:answer/id #uuid "089c44cb-1411-5b96-a011-181bffcdb31b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383427} {:answer/id #uuid "0538b559-3b27-53cf-bf4b-40d7ba06c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} {:answer/id #uuid "7e08020a-0701-57c1-8997-c2b48f3e8f20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383429} {:answer/id #uuid "f1836749-28a7-5fbd-bd3f-d78ad98340b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0538b559-3b27-53cf-bf4b-40d7ba06c7ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383428} :answer-field/id #uuid "352c429f-c09f-51de-981a-03fd6b374654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383425}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5c458b4-8436-5a2e-a1dd-0c56afc161a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-139540" :question/problem "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{3},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={7}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={7}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=7.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ade149-2321-54bc-8689-575d0021aa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400772} {:answer/id #uuid "0322f6ef-5cd8-5b08-95d5-f8a6bea015f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400773} {:answer/id #uuid "56994b9b-ba0c-523d-9995-ad7e4cb025c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9{v}_{1}$ and $4{v}_{1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400774} {:answer/id #uuid "caa9e423-3397-56e4-b07f-3555e9f94692" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400775} {:answer/id #uuid "cc058b2e-f11f-5130-a8ae-a5e6d87d4307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47ade149-2321-54bc-8689-575d0021aa81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400772} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b9d051-ab23-5d38-8461-85040e182756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3e183c0-f2c5-57a2-8b6d-0c81e407e953" :question/math-academy-id "q-139550" :question/problem "![](images/57/57368434b3025dc777c5be725da43e365dfd8ef3c47049570c0ecb13f2baf035.png)\n\nA $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ what are the nonzero singular values of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=9={\\sigma }_{1},$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=4={\\sigma }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162098 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9cec3c21-28df-52ba-9b78-86cffb4c7720" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems Regarding the Image of the Unit Eigenvectors of a Quadratic Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be3ba996-d9c0-57d3-95cc-5f43b21decb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383433} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbea375-60a4-5dc4-b6be-dd9b80d209fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383434} {:answer/id #uuid "85175ea8-b77f-56c3-83e4-10dd48da9dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383435} {:answer/id #uuid "98ae820c-445e-5ef8-bf8b-2b43811699d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383436} {:answer/id #uuid "0a9af6f2-c869-5f00-9c07-d01d9bfe39e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{A}^{T}{v}_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bbea375-60a4-5dc4-b6be-dd9b80d209fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{v}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383434} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c7c6fc9-8fb7-527f-ae85-2cdf815c3fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "caf843b3-f2a1-5bdc-8d57-74ed37aa0fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-139554" :question/problem "![](images/63/63d5de8c08559ecc88b4fd7e2184204a1e6ea2f8a9e8ca6af7959e2354876e3f.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $9$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={5}^{2}=25,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={3}^{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$"}]}
